WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:04.910
SCOTT G. WEINER: Prescription drug monitoring programs

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:09.420
are very powerful databases that currently 49 states have implemented.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.520
It's a pretty new thing.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.090
They have just started in the past-- maybe five years or so for most states,

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:17.630
although a handful of states have had them for a couple of decades.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.360
The new thing about this is that they are often web-based.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.390
So as an emergency physician, I can see a patient.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.540
If I'm considering giving them a medication, like an opioid,

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.670
I can log on to a state database.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:31.640
And what it does is it tells me all of the controlled substance

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.380
prescriptions-- those are medications like benzodiazepines for anxiety,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.720
or pain medications like opioids--

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.210
tells me every single prescription they've had in the state

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.810
and often in surrounding states now as well too.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.480
This has been a game changer for us.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.740
There's so-called doctor shopping, where patients go from hospital to hospital.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.040
We don't like that term but that's often frequently what it's called.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.230
And we've seen a marked decrease in that because

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.840
of the existence of prescription drug monitoring programs.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.105
And the reason is that it becomes difficult to have multiple prescribers

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:04.980
for your medications.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.230
So patients that go from emergency department to emergency department

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:13.250
and get different prescriptions now have difficulty with that, frankly,

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.219
and that's a good thing.

00:01:15.219 --> 00:01:17.510
It's gotten to the point where initially we were really

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.620
advocating for them to be implemented, and now the government has

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.750
realized how powerful these are.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.850
And in many states it's actually mandated.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.629
So in the state of Massachusetts, for example,

00:01:28.629 --> 00:01:31.670
if I want to prescribe a prescription-- if I want to prescribe an opioid,

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.350
I have to access the state prescription drug monitoring program at that point.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.390
It's forced us to use it.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.960
It's actually entered our workflow, which is a very good thing.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.960
And probably the most powerful part about this

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.930
is that if I'm seeing a patient that has a profile that

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:52.220
has lots of prescriptions from lots of different providers, I print that out,

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.390
and I bring it to the bedside, and it starts a conversation.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.110
Why do you have so many providers?

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.880
Why are you getting so many prescriptions?

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.640
And that can be a very powerful teachable moment.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.880
SARAH O'NEILL: I think pharmacists play a very big role.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.669
Number one because we're responsible for dispensing the opioids.

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:11.820
So the opioids have to come from somewhere.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:18.200
And although many of them are found throughout a prescription,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:23.220
usually they are obtained by someone who had a prescription from their doctor

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.470
and they got it at a pharmacy.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:29.750
So it's our job as pharmacists to make sure that every prescription we fill

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.640
is for a legitimate medical purpose, and it's written by a doctor that

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.880
is practicing within their scope.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:43.490
And so it is our job to prevent any prescriptions being filled that

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:44.550
shouldn't.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.770
Basically having patients who are in pain, that need the medication, get

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:55.160
it, and the other patients that are potentially abusing the drug

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:59.990
or selling it, hopefully they wouldn't be getting it from the pharmacy.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:04.820
We also help avoid the opioid crisis by maintaining

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.860
an accurate inventory in the pharmacy.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:11.030
Make sure there's no diversion or stealing, as you would call it.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:15.710
And if anyone calls and they ask, hey, how much oxycodone do you have?

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.140
We don't give them that information over the phone.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.760
We keep very strict inventory.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.620
So there's many ways, including making sure

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:28.790
that we check the prescription monitoring program and communicating

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.800
with doctors, if there's any patient that's

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.410
trying to fill prescriptions early, or anything

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.100
that seems out of the ordinary.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.820
So there's many ways that I feel that we can

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.480
help continue to decrease the crisis.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.050
Sometimes we're faced with a situation in the pharmacy

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:49.340
where we do not fill an opioid medication when

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:52.700
the patient brings the prescription, even though the doctor wrote it.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.360
There are several reasons that this may occur.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.870
One of the most common reasons is that it's too soon to fill it.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.380
We have a very strict policy as far as when

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:08.450
you can feel your controlled substances to avoid anybody getting early refills.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.930
So typically if you filled your prescription

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:19.579
for your controlled substance 30 days ago, basically,

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:24.060
we would never fill it sooner than 28 days from that time.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.070
So we have to make sure not to fill any prescriptions early

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.790
that are controlled substances, and that's

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.910
the number one reason why we would deny a patient filling

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.820
a medication for a controlled substance.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.730
Other reasons that we might not fill a prescription

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:44.030
is because of other red flags that we see in their profile.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:46.280
They don't want to fill it through their insurance,

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:51.200
for example, which is a red flag because everybody has insurance.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:52.460
It's required.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.340
And basically, if they're saying, I don't

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:59.570
want to pay for it with my insurance, they're

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:04.120
trying to avoid us seeing them getting another prescription, most likely

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.500
somewhere else, where did go through the insurance.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.270
So in this case they're usually pharmacy shopping or doctor shopping.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:14.170
And in this case, it's easy now for us to check on the prescription monitoring

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:17.740
program online and see where they filled their last prescription.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:18.700
How many they got?

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.650
Are they do for a refill?

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.790
It's our job, basically, to make sure that if you have pain medication,

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:27.250
you get it when you're due to fill it.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:33.550
Not too soon, not before it's due, but you should be taking as prescribed.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:36.130
And in that case, you shouldn't need to get it early.

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.610
So if a patient tells me that they're out

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:43.240
because they were taking it differently than what the label said,

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.270
that concerns me and I immediately contact the doctor.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.727
We play a big role in keeping the doctor in the loop

00:05:49.727 --> 00:05:52.060
because a lot of information comes from the patient when

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.480
they're trying to get a prescription and it's, for instance, too soon

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.790
to be filled.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:01.900
We can also not fill a prescription if after we talk to the doctor,

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.700
they say, hey, I didn't know that they had this other prescription.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.000
They never told me.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.480
Cancel the prescription.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.550
So sometimes the doctors do in fact cancel the prescriptions

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.250
that they already wrote once they're informed about the other prescriptions

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:17.200
that they got.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:20.890
These days, that's mostly avoided because they've already

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:24.580
seen before they wrote the prescription, by logging into the prescription

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:28.300
monitoring program, what they've got and the last time they got it.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:32.260
So that issue doesn't really fall on the pharmacist anymore.

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:34.540
But sometimes it falls through the cracks

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:37.107
and a patient is using more than one doctor,

00:06:37.107 --> 00:06:39.190
and maybe they're outside of the partners network.

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:43.240
And that's the situation where we don't feel comfortable

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:46.960
filling the prescription, but we keep the doctor in the loop as well.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:51.460
The MedSafe program is, basically, a disposal unit

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:54.400
that sits outside the pharmacy at Mass General.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.220
And it allows patients, at any time of day,

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.687
to come and drop off their medications.

00:07:00.687 --> 00:07:03.020
Whether it has a label on it or it doesn't have a label,

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:06.670
they're not sure what it is, it doesn't matter as long as it's not a syringe.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:10.630
Any type of tablet at all can be disposed here,

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:13.150
and it just gets incinerated.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:17.530
And it's safely handled by our employees in the pharmacy.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:22.330
We dispose of the box and then the MedSafe company incinerates it.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:28.330
So they don't need to worry about their personal information being anywhere.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:29.920
So it's very safe.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.370
And here at Mass General, it's so popular

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:38.840
that we have to change out the unit about once a week.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:42.340
So there's a lot of medications out there that are being dropped off.

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.810
So it's been a very effective form of drug take back.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:51.520
Every week we have to replace the disposal unit.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.230
And we send back about 50 pounds worth of drugs back to the company

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:58.540
to be incinerated, just to give you an idea

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:02.740
of how many drugs are actually dropped off every week here at the hospital.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.910
It's a very significant amount, and it decreases

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:10.810
the number of tablets that are out in people's

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:14.350
homes and available for basically use.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.880
And it could be controls and non-controls.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.290
So it's not just controlled substances that are going in there.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:25.440
It's any type of old, expired, unused medication.

